WEBVTT

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.851
[Kaspar]: You are listening to Clap to COLAB at Colaboradio.

00:00:15.205 --> 00:00:24.407
[Kaspar]: This is a part of Freie Radios Berlin Brandenburg, and streaming is on fr.f-bb.org and Colaboradio.

00:00:25.605 --> 00:00:33.650
[Kaspar]: If you're listening to a radio with DAB plus please tune into channel seven d for Berlin and channel 12D for Brandenburg.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:45.282
[Kaspar]: This is a special radio show and I'm sound artist Kaspar König, and I present interviews and sound recordings from the project called COLAB Technobiological Futures.

00:00:46.025 --> 00:00:50.352
[Kaspar]: A project by the artist run bio and hacking labs hosted on Hackteria.

00:00:50.585 --> 00:00:55.005
[Kaspar]: So definitely check out www.hacktaria.org.

00:00:55.625 --> 00:01:13.715
[Kaspar]: So in this show, I try to gather the information on different events and different, celebrations of this collaborative lab culture that we want to not only promote, but is also supported by Pro Helvetia and their synergy grant.

00:01:14.415 --> 00:01:21.635
[Kaspar]: All of what we do in, the Colaboration should be or could be in the DIWO spirit, do it with others.

00:01:22.335 --> 00:01:27.200
[Kaspar]: So we try to collaborate and everybody has another definition of collaboration.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:47.855
[Kaspar]: So that's where also this program is a little bit about what can we figure out on maybe remote collaborations or international networks, or maybe even super local localism as in workshopology that, is part of many of our practices.

00:01:49.195 --> 00:01:53.880
[Kaspar]: We start off with a small recording in ear dot education.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:02:04.120
[Kaspar]: I think I remember right when it, was two years ago on a birthday party and people just started to jam.

00:02:04.775 --> 00:02:08.155
[Kaspar]: And that's something of a collaboration that is amazing.

00:04:51.056 --> 00:04:53.300
[Darcy]: Shetty sunny here, so I could show you some sunshine.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.260
[Kaspar]: I'm now blending in to the first interview guest.

00:04:59.205 --> 00:04:59.705
[Darcy]: Absolutely.

00:05:00.005 --> 00:05:00.405
[Darcy]: Yeah.

00:05:00.405 --> 00:05:02.185
[Darcy]: So my name is Darcy Neal.

00:05:02.485 --> 00:05:13.940
[Darcy]: I am a creative technologist and workshopologist who who works in, specifically with hardware electronics as my main thing.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.400
[Darcy]: So everything that I do, it tends to have a little bit of that in it.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:19.780
[Darcy]: I'm really interested in ex...

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:32.305
[Darcy]: In programming interactivity into my designs, and so I like exploring the experimental ways that we can work with electronics and especially synthesizers and other things like that.

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:53.320
[Darcy]: The the main bread and butter of my work and the workshops that I've been doing for, I think, about the past two and a half years at this point has been all about exploring electromagnetic frequencies and tapping into ways to listen in on the otherwise invisible world that surrounds us.

00:07:01.265 --> 00:07:01.585
[Darcy]: Yeah.

00:07:01.585 --> 00:07:12.270
[Darcy]: The thing that grows best in my lab is non organic materials and giant piles of random wires and components and things like that.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:12.970
[Kaspar]: Yeah.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:24.055
[Darcy]: So, there is some, like, I do some experiments with, like, trying to make, like, conductive leather, you know, and different experimental surfaces.

00:07:24.595 --> 00:07:27.875
[Darcy]: But for the most part, I'm dealing it with the...

00:07:27.875 --> 00:07:32.375
[Darcy]: With it in whatever biological components in their dried state.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:33.370
[Kaspar]: Good.

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:36.750
[Darcy]: So it's mostly just a mess of wires for the most part.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:37.130
[Darcy]: Okay.

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:40.890
[Kaspar]: Could you describe a little bit what what happens when you enter it, or is it

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:41.790
[Darcy]: Totally.

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:42.830
[Darcy]: It it...

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:44.650
[Darcy]: It's always in a state of flux.

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:48.590
[Darcy]: There's always a project going, usually about six of them at the same time.

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:50.755
[Darcy]: And so I've organized my lab and...

00:07:50.755 --> 00:07:52.055
[Darcy]: As a system of...

00:07:52.515 --> 00:07:55.255
[Darcy]: I use these cafeteria trays, actually.

00:07:55.795 --> 00:07:59.395
[Darcy]: And so each tray has a dedicated project on it.

00:07:59.395 --> 00:08:04.215
[Darcy]: It's like the baker's tray format, which I think that a lot of other artists like to use.

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:14.950
[Darcy]: And that's great because even if my desk is messy, I could just put the tray on top of the mess, and then I have a new project that I'm working on, and then I can just pick the whole pile up and put it away.

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:27.045
[Darcy]: And, like, all of my surface mount components, I keep it in a baseball card divider book, whereas in each little pocket, I cut out the label for the Darcy, and so I can flip through the pages, and I keep it organized.

00:08:27.825 --> 00:08:29.105
[Darcy]: That is my ideal state.

00:08:29.105 --> 00:08:34.820
[Darcy]: In in reality, the surface mount components are in a big box labeled to do, to be labeled.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:35.300
[Darcy]: You know?

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:39.320
[Darcy]: So, it's always in a state of flux one way or another.

00:09:44.895 --> 00:09:49.535
[Kaspar]: Converting also to maybe Colaboradio you do, are they in a flux as well?

00:09:49.535 --> 00:09:57.715
[Kaspar]: Is this your beloved state of, working together, or what is collaboration for you, and how do you tangle it?

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:14.555
[Darcy]: I would say that collaboration for me, it varies, you know, because, like, for example, I have an ongoing collaboration that I did with some folks that has been going on for about five plus years now.

00:10:14.935 --> 00:10:22.795
[Darcy]: It was for a large interactive synthesizer controller that I built that controls a visual and audio synthesizer.

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:26.420
[Darcy]: And so that project is ongoing.

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:28.280
[Darcy]: It's been about five years for that.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:41.285
[Darcy]: But I've also been in several, artist collectives as well where we are all working together as a team, and that's actually my preferred state is working with a group of other artists.

00:10:41.745 --> 00:10:42.245
[Kaspar]: Mhmm.

00:10:42.625 --> 00:10:43.745
[Darcy]: And so it's just

00:10:43.985 --> 00:10:45.125
[Kaspar]: Why is that?

00:10:45.985 --> 00:10:47.925
[Darcy]: It's just so fun.

00:10:48.225 --> 00:10:58.130
[Darcy]: It's like I like feeding off of the energy of other people being excited because a lot of times, it's just me being excited about something that most other people don't understand.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:10:59.170
[Darcy]: You know?

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:14.045
[Darcy]: And so to find other people who are interested in the same weird niche ideas and are interested in exploring the same topics, it is so nice to be around other folks who share that enthusiasm.

00:11:14.345 --> 00:11:19.010
[Darcy]: Like, I feed off of that off of that drive and that creativity.

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:20.050
[Darcy]: It's just...

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:23.490
[Darcy]: So it's really special to me whenever I can work with other individuals.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:25.810
[Kaspar]: How how do you see the future of collaboration?

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:28.930
[Darcy]: How do I see the future of collaboration?

00:11:29.825 --> 00:11:34.725
[Darcy]: It's so interesting because we're in such a changing world right now, and it's like...

00:11:35.665 --> 00:11:55.310
[Darcy]: It feels like it feels like that, like, that same drive that I have to be around my people, you know, and to be in community with other individuals who share the same interests, it feels like there's parts of the world that is actively working to dismantle that ability to get together.

00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:59.710
[Darcy]: And I'm from The US, so I feel it quite heavily right now.

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:01.150
[Darcy]: And so...

00:12:02.355 --> 00:12:14.535
[Darcy]: And I think that that is something that is to be kept in mind as we move forward with these collaborations that our community is more important than ever, and so it will take a...

00:12:14.675 --> 00:12:20.030
[Darcy]: It does take additional work in order to keep to keep like minded folks together.

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:36.325
[Darcy]: And so I think a lot of the collaboration for me, like, some of it has to happen digitally, where we have, you know, different groups that we can gather together, like, whether it's on, like, or other, you know, private chats and whatnot.

00:12:37.505 --> 00:12:59.890
[Darcy]: But then also for the way that I've been working to find communities in order to do projects together is I've been doing these workshops that I've been focusing on for the past year and a half to two years, because it allows for me to travel to the different maker and hacker events where I can go and be amongst my people.

00:13:00.445 --> 00:13:06.865
[Darcy]: And it's just a wonderful opportunity to celebrate our to celebrate our time together.

00:13:07.005 --> 00:13:17.280
[Darcy]: And so I think that as things have shifted and the meeting spaces have sometimes gone away.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:33.715
[Darcy]: That traveling to go and be at specific events where we can gather together for, you know, anywhere from three days to one week to two weeks together, that's kind of been the way that I've been working to make sure that I can still be amongst my people.

00:13:35.215 --> 00:13:40.195
[Darcy]: But so much of it is, like, being really good at digital communication is really helpful.

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:47.060
[Darcy]: In order to make sure that, like, we can work on projects as a group and actually, like, make progress on it.

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:48.120
[Darcy]: So Yeah.

00:14:21.025 --> 00:14:26.645
[Darcy]: It was really incredible in order to be together in a group in Chiang Mai.

00:14:27.105 --> 00:14:29.265
[Darcy]: It was everything.

00:14:29.265 --> 00:14:31.925
[Darcy]: It was everything that I wanted it to be.

00:14:32.305 --> 00:14:40.750
[Darcy]: It's like everything that I want in terms of, like, artistic Colaboradio, it was all there.

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:53.435
[Darcy]: And so being able to stay in the same space and we all share the same studio and we all had access to, like, the same common work areas, it was really, really nice, because...

00:14:53.435 --> 00:14:54.335
[Darcy]: And it's just...

00:14:54.475 --> 00:14:58.095
[Darcy]: We were immersed in this creative environment.

00:14:58.955 --> 00:14:59.275
[Darcy]: You know?

00:14:59.275 --> 00:15:00.395
[Darcy]: And then we are a lot of times...

00:15:00.395 --> 00:15:06.090
[Darcy]: We're cooking meals together too, so it's just like everything that we do is an act of community.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:14.490
[Darcy]: And so it was really incredible for that, and we're surrounded by Futures, like there's just biodiversity everywhere.

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:16.490
[Darcy]: It was just an absolute dream.

00:15:47.995 --> 00:15:48.475
[Darcy]: I don't...

00:15:48.475 --> 00:15:49.535
[Darcy]: I love Berlin.

00:15:49.595 --> 00:15:50.735
[Darcy]: I love going there.

00:15:51.915 --> 00:16:10.180
[Darcy]: And one of the original inspirations for doing the workshops that I do today was specifically going to the chaos communication camp in 2023, and that's when I really saw the scale of the soldering workshops and how they could be developed.

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:18.355
[Darcy]: And so the workshops that I've created since then have been specifically designed to work in those kinds of environments.

00:16:18.815 --> 00:16:26.195
[Darcy]: Because I think that being able to work on technology in an outdoor space is just so good for the soul.

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:29.570
[Darcy]: Because, like, I'm locked inside of my lab most of the time.

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:31.030
[Darcy]: I don't see that much sunlight.

00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:33.110
[Darcy]: I see it through the window sometimes.

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:33.750
[Darcy]: You know?

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:34.290
[Darcy]: Mhmm.

00:16:35.650 --> 00:16:42.555
[Darcy]: But I think that those particular kinds of environments really can help to feed the creativity and feed the soul.

00:16:42.555 --> 00:16:44.315
[Darcy]: So I just really...

00:16:44.315 --> 00:16:45.855
[Darcy]: I'm really grateful for it.

00:17:31.930 --> 00:17:32.250
[Darcy]: Yeah.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:33.530
[Darcy]: Thank you so much for everything.

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:35.505
[Darcy]: It was wonderful to talk to you.

00:17:35.725 --> 00:17:38.385
[Kaspar]: Thank you, Darcia, for the insights.

00:17:39.005 --> 00:17:47.105
[Kaspar]: Now before we go on, Yashas Shetty, I would love to play some music from the map here.

00:17:47.245 --> 00:17:47.745
[Kaspar]: Zurich.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:20.980
[Yashas]: Hi.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:22.360
[Yashas]: So my name is Yashas.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:27.480
[Yashas]: I live in India, in a city called Bangalore.

00:19:28.835 --> 00:19:31.655
[Yashas]: So we've got, a festival.

00:19:32.515 --> 00:19:35.175
[Yashas]: So today and tomorrow is a holiday.

00:19:37.235 --> 00:19:41.015
[Yashas]: So it's a big festival for this part of the country.

00:19:41.395 --> 00:19:45.180
[Yashas]: So, I think it's eight or nine days.

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:54.940
[Yashas]: So, but officially we get tomorrow off, but I took a long weekend off.

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:58.585
[Yashas]: This state made it into a giant ritual.

00:19:58.645 --> 00:20:00.985
[Yashas]: So there's like elephants in procession.

00:20:01.445 --> 00:20:03.845
[Yashas]: I mean, we're lucky to have a lot of these things.

00:20:03.845 --> 00:20:05.225
[Yashas]: So you get days off.

00:20:06.245 --> 00:20:19.030
[Yashas]: Interestingly, however, tomorrow is, the holiday for worshiping or praying or giving respect to your tools.

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:26.785
[Yashas]: So every tool that you have, so I might do it tomorrow.

00:20:26.845 --> 00:20:31.185
[Yashas]: So all the synthesizers, you give thanks to your synthesizers.

00:20:32.445 --> 00:20:33.485
[Yashas]: Everybody does it.

00:20:33.485 --> 00:20:37.665
[Yashas]: So whatever tools you have, it's a day for tools.

00:20:37.725 --> 00:20:44.080
[Yashas]: So you you you say, yeah, you say, you know, give thanks to them.

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:44.860
[Yashas]: Yeah.

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:48.400
[Yashas]: It's called in, in the local language, it's called Ayuda puja.

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:51.440
[Yashas]: So Ayuda has different meanings.

00:20:52.485 --> 00:20:56.985
[Yashas]: So one one obvious meaning is weapon, but it also means tools.

00:20:57.125 --> 00:20:59.365
[Yashas]: Ayuda is your tool that you use.

00:20:59.365 --> 00:21:09.510
[Yashas]: So tomorrow is Ayuda puja, which means you kind of, you know, you say, you know, thanks for helping me out.

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:13.030
[Kaspar]: What, is your COLAB about?

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:16.870
[Yashas]: For a living, I teach in an art school.

00:21:17.265 --> 00:21:20.005
[Yashas]: I, so that's how I make my living.

00:21:20.945 --> 00:21:23.845
[Yashas]: I work with, different collectives.

00:21:25.745 --> 00:21:35.750
[Yashas]: One collective that I work for is called the Indian Sonic Research Organization, where we look at sound, different aspects of sound.

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:46.470
[Yashas]: We look at music, we have a space for workshops, we do concerts, we have a little studio, it's an open studio.

00:21:47.715 --> 00:21:49.575
[Yashas]: We do events and residencies.

00:21:51.715 --> 00:22:05.410
[Yashas]: I guess the reason why I'm talking to you right now is also because of the Hackteria network, which I guess is a part of the program that you're doing.

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:25.005
[Yashas]: So, me, Mark, and another artist called Andy Garcia started that in 2009, which was also kind of exploring the ideas of what is a collective, what does it mean to collaborate.

00:22:26.265 --> 00:22:31.485
[Yashas]: We don't have answers to those questions, but, we were exploring those questions.

00:22:31.625 --> 00:22:36.240
[Yashas]: So, so I guess that's who I am in a sense.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:42.580
[Yashas]: I guess, I'm just another dude who's just trying to do what he likes to do.

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:04.360
[Yashas]: So I work in multiple...

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:06.645
[Yashas]: I work primarily in two labs.

00:29:07.205 --> 00:29:11.225
[Yashas]: I work in a lab called art art science lab or art science Bangalore.

00:29:12.005 --> 00:29:24.660
[Yashas]: That was kind of the first lab that I set up, and that's in the art school, that primarily looks at, collaborations between artists and scientists.

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:30.740
[Yashas]: And it is a lab that is within the...

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:35.475
[Yashas]: Within an institutional framework, so that is inside another institution.

00:29:36.675 --> 00:29:36.995
[Yashas]: Institution.

00:29:37.315 --> 00:29:39.495
[Yashas]: And then I also work at ISRO.

00:29:40.355 --> 00:29:46.855
[Yashas]: So I guess with both the labs, your question is what grows best.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:53.650
[Yashas]: I think, hopefully, I would like to say that people grow best in our labs.

00:29:54.830 --> 00:30:05.515
[Yashas]: We have lots of people coming in and out, and hopefully, when they enter a laboratory, they change in some way.

00:30:07.095 --> 00:30:07.255
[Yashas]: So...

00:30:07.255 --> 00:30:13.035
[Yashas]: And then people, when they grow, they bring in ideas that grow or ideas that change.

00:30:14.135 --> 00:30:15.340
[Yashas]: So I guess the primary...

00:30:15.340 --> 00:30:22.960
[Yashas]: For me, at least, the primary objective of a lab is to have people grow inside it.

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:30.080
[Yashas]: So so the the question is what is a techno biological future?

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:33.815
[Yashas]: Is that the question?

00:30:34.595 --> 00:30:35.095
[Yashas]: Yeah.

00:30:35.235 --> 00:30:35.715
[Yashas]: Okay.

00:30:38.195 --> 00:30:39.735
[Yashas]: I guess I...

00:30:40.115 --> 00:30:42.695
[Yashas]: Again, this is my perspective on it.

00:30:44.100 --> 00:31:00.755
[Yashas]: If you want to look to the future, you'll have to look definitely to the past to see what went on in the past, and then kind of not necessarily extrapolate because that's stupid, and that never works out.

00:31:01.615 --> 00:31:05.795
[Yashas]: I can only speculate what what is going on.

00:31:06.895 --> 00:31:13.475
[Yashas]: So, obviously, I'm sure you'd agree that we live in difficult times now around the world.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:31.035
[Yashas]: So this could be the future, but it's slightly difficult from different perspectives, environmental perspective, technological, biological, political.

00:31:33.735 --> 00:31:39.175
[Yashas]: There is trouble already and that this trouble might persist, and that is...

00:31:39.175 --> 00:31:40.955
[Yashas]: Could be one kind of future.

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:48.910
[Yashas]: So all of your Technobiological future could imply some kind of trouble.

00:31:50.570 --> 00:32:02.725
[Yashas]: So, that's a pessimist way of looking at things, but being a pessimist is obviously very boring, as is being a techno optimist.

00:32:02.865 --> 00:32:04.245
[Yashas]: That is also boring.

00:32:06.385 --> 00:32:07.285
[Yashas]: So I guess...

00:32:08.385 --> 00:32:09.125
[Yashas]: Well, my...

00:32:09.345 --> 00:32:22.870
[Yashas]: Again, I'm not sure if I'm answering your question, but a Technobiological future has some elements of the past and has some elements of our present in the future.

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:36.695
[Yashas]: It might not be radically different unless there's some kind of nuclear war or environmental catastrophe, which is happening in the sense of environmental catastrophe.

00:32:37.075 --> 00:32:57.585
[Yashas]: But, again, with respect to Hackteria, we did think about what the future of this interface between the biological, living environment, living organisms and the technological environment is.

00:32:57.585 --> 00:33:00.305
[Yashas]: So that was primarily our question.

00:33:00.305 --> 00:33:02.485
[Yashas]: What would this look like?

00:33:02.865 --> 00:33:17.230
[Yashas]: And I would say for most part, we were right in the sense, a lot of the members of Hackteria in in in the two thousands would call themselves biohackers.

00:33:18.745 --> 00:33:25.485
[Yashas]: And then now every moron in Silicon Valley calls himself for...

00:33:25.545 --> 00:33:39.270
[Yashas]: Is primarily himself, very rarely herself, a biohacker, just completely disrupting its meaning, co opting it, and messing it up.

00:33:39.730 --> 00:33:51.835
[Yashas]: So now it means, you know, search for immortality, search for perfection, search for health, none of which we were thinking about.

00:33:51.895 --> 00:33:52.295
[Yashas]: Right?

00:33:52.295 --> 00:33:52.615
[Yashas]: It's...

00:33:52.615 --> 00:34:02.790
[Yashas]: I mean, we were thinking about these things, but then, again, from a historical perspective, from the perspective of alchemy, things like that.

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:08.870
[Yashas]: So things from the past, the search for immortality as ancient as humans themselves.

00:34:10.050 --> 00:34:23.705
[Yashas]: But then, you know, it turns into this weird posthuman future that people in California, Silicon Valley, you know, impose on us.

00:34:24.245 --> 00:34:37.290
[Yashas]: So hopefully a techno biological future could have ideas that are not dominated by these people and are dominated by ideas from other parts of the world.

00:34:37.670 --> 00:34:39.370
[Yashas]: So that could be one future.

00:34:42.435 --> 00:34:51.335
[Yashas]: And of course, one would hope that a future would be a just and an equal world for everyone.

00:34:53.315 --> 00:34:55.255
[Yashas]: So that's what one hopes for.

00:34:56.730 --> 00:35:04.830
[Yashas]: And that's all, again, old hope, a whole old dream of humanity of a just Futures, equal future for everyone.

00:35:06.010 --> 00:35:26.535
[Yashas]: So in this sense, I would say a Technobiological future is not very different from a future all humans hope for themselves, right, to be free, to live in a just world, to live in an equal world, to live in a world where everyone has opportunities.

00:35:27.155 --> 00:35:32.680
[Yashas]: So I think, hopefully, the technological future, it will include this as well.

00:43:20.670 --> 00:43:22.630
[Kaspar]: Realm of collaboration.

00:43:23.230 --> 00:43:25.890
[Kaspar]: What what do you need to to collaborate?

00:43:28.245 --> 00:43:46.270
[Yashas]: So first I kind of, you know, at least I've been hanging out with scientists for about twenty, twenty five odd years now, working with all of the people in Hackteria here in Bangalore, And I've changed my mind on this kind of ideas of neutrality.

00:43:46.570 --> 00:43:48.430
[Yashas]: There is no neutral science.

00:43:48.490 --> 00:43:50.590
[Yashas]: There is no neutral algorithm.

00:43:50.890 --> 00:43:52.570
[Yashas]: Obviously, all of us live through it.

00:43:52.570 --> 00:43:56.065
[Yashas]: All the algorithms are biased with human prejudices.

00:43:56.365 --> 00:43:56.765
[Yashas]: Right?

00:43:56.765 --> 00:44:01.085
[Yashas]: With our cultural prejudices, the machine is not neutral.

00:44:01.085 --> 00:44:02.465
[Yashas]: The machine is biased.

00:44:03.725 --> 00:44:05.105
[Yashas]: Science is not neutral.

00:44:05.325 --> 00:44:07.185
[Yashas]: Science has an agenda.

00:44:07.245 --> 00:44:08.545
[Yashas]: Science is biased.

00:44:08.605 --> 00:44:14.750
[Yashas]: No matter how how how much we would like for it to be objective, there is no such thing.

00:44:15.290 --> 00:44:21.302
[Yashas]: Again, for me, a hopeful future is to look at the machine as a tool, as a collaborator.

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:34.115
[Yashas]: Maybe as a collaborator, but definitely as a tool rather than kind of have all of these, you know, ideas about machines taking over, etcetera, etcetera.

00:44:34.255 --> 00:44:39.715
[Yashas]: They might be taking over in terms of economics, in terms of politics.

00:44:40.950 --> 00:44:50.410
[Yashas]: There are still tools that hopefully you can, you have the freedom to mold and manipulate and do what you want to do.

00:44:50.790 --> 00:45:08.245
[Yashas]: And right now, a lot of these tools are being used by large corporations or giant institutions like the state, you know, to spy on us, to surveil us, to, get rid of labor, for example.

00:45:11.150 --> 00:45:18.430
[Yashas]: So it would be good if we go back to the idea that, you know, this is a tool and has to...

00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:21.285
[Yashas]: We have to learn how to use it, and then we use it.

00:45:22.325 --> 00:45:24.265
[Yashas]: And then, again, the goals...

00:45:24.965 --> 00:45:43.350
[Yashas]: If our goals were that we want equal world, we want freedom for humans, then we must make use of the tool, learn how to use it, see how it works, open up its black box because everything is now a black box.

00:45:44.530 --> 00:45:48.645
[Yashas]: And when you open a black box, you find another little black box.

00:45:48.805 --> 00:45:49.305
[Yashas]: Right?

00:45:50.725 --> 00:46:14.765
[Yashas]: And most of our time as, you know, in Hackteria as hackers or as artists is, you know, trying to open up a little black box and see where it fails because that's the most interesting thing, and see how you can, you know, use it in a creative way, for for example.

00:46:15.065 --> 00:46:28.845
[Yashas]: So, and right now, everything that our lives are in black boxes, we don't know how these rules are made, why we are following them, why this decision or that decision is made.

00:46:30.880 --> 00:46:40.720
[Yashas]: So again, back to the previous question, a hopeful future would be one where we have seized control much like the Luddites tried to do.

00:46:40.720 --> 00:46:45.975
[Yashas]: You know, they have a bad name, but they were all masters of their machines.

00:46:47.555 --> 00:46:57.495
[Yashas]: The Luddites in England, you know, the ones that in the industrial revolution went in and broke all the machines, and they they were given a bad name.

00:46:57.635 --> 00:46:58.135
[Yashas]: They...

00:46:58.435 --> 00:47:01.210
[Yashas]: We were told that they hated the machines.

00:47:01.210 --> 00:47:01.770
[Yashas]: They didn't.

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:04.430
[Yashas]: They were the actual operators of the machine.

00:47:05.130 --> 00:47:06.590
[Yashas]: They knew how it worked.

00:47:07.610 --> 00:47:14.590
[Yashas]: I think seizing control of our tools is an important part of our future.

00:47:15.675 --> 00:47:20.715
[Yashas]: And I I think the Hackteria played a a huge part in that.

00:47:20.715 --> 00:47:20.955
[Yashas]: Right?

00:47:20.955 --> 00:47:24.975
[Yashas]: Making these tools of biology accessible to people.

00:47:25.035 --> 00:47:41.000
[Yashas]: To celebrate your tools and be thankful to them, but, and hope they will not take control of you by respecting them and trying to understand them and, treating them with respect.

00:47:42.075 --> 00:47:48.975
[Yashas]: But now I think the future that is presented to us is that they will be in control and you will not be in control.

00:47:49.755 --> 00:47:52.735
[Yashas]: Again, control is a controversial word.

00:47:52.875 --> 00:47:53.375
[Kaspar]: Yes.

00:47:53.515 --> 00:47:54.015
[Kaspar]: Again?

00:47:54.395 --> 00:48:00.210
[Kaspar]: We are seven one thousandth of a degree above absolute zero.

00:48:01.630 --> 00:48:02.130
[Kaspar]: Amazing.

00:48:07.550 --> 00:48:10.130
[Kaspar]: It's just pushing the black box, as you said.

00:48:10.795 --> 00:48:27.790
[Kaspar]: So one last question maybe on the interview would be collaboration as in what is the smallest entity or what is the easiest way to enter that, realm of collaboration?

00:48:29.050 --> 00:48:31.710
[Kaspar]: What what do you need to to collaborate?

00:48:33.770 --> 00:48:39.275
[Yashas]: So when you kind of started this, talk and you told me about clap for collaboration.

00:48:39.895 --> 00:48:41.675
[Yashas]: The word CLAP is interesting.

00:48:42.935 --> 00:48:44.395
[Yashas]: Just a small anecdote.

00:48:44.535 --> 00:48:54.630
[Yashas]: I was, in Stuttgart for a fellowship many years ago, And this fellowship had people from different disciplines, right?

00:48:54.630 --> 00:49:00.650
[Yashas]: So there were architects, and there were urban planners, and there were visual artists.

00:49:01.270 --> 00:49:02.810
[Yashas]: And then there were the musicians.

00:49:03.635 --> 00:49:10.455
[Yashas]: Now the musicians, the architects would say to they would tell another architect, let's collaborate.

00:49:11.875 --> 00:49:23.200
[Yashas]: And that involved them, trying to write a proposal grant proposal to get grant money so that they could collaborate in the future.

00:49:23.980 --> 00:49:29.600
[Yashas]: But the musicians would say, let's just come to my room and let's just let's just jam.

00:49:30.995 --> 00:49:32.715
[Yashas]: I think they didn't need anything.

00:49:32.715 --> 00:49:36.655
[Yashas]: They just they they just wanted the other person with them.

00:49:37.355 --> 00:49:41.355
[Yashas]: So I think so that that that was always a funny moment for me.

00:49:41.355 --> 00:49:43.135
[Yashas]: I always thought about that.

00:49:43.195 --> 00:49:54.370
[Yashas]: Every other discipline, let's collaborate would mean Shetty kind of pull up papers and pencils out and think of how we want to collaborate in the future.

00:49:55.310 --> 00:50:02.205
[Yashas]: Whereas for a musician, it was just just come to my room, get a bottle of beer and we collaborate, right?

00:50:02.205 --> 00:50:20.670
[Yashas]: So I think that spirit spread to other disciplines or other people. collaboration is just bring some food, bring up, bring something to drink, and we talk and we see what happens.

00:50:20.970 --> 00:50:21.370
[Yashas]: Right?

00:50:21.370 --> 00:50:23.070
[Yashas]: It doesn't even have to work.

00:50:23.585 --> 00:50:24.085
[Yashas]: It...

00:50:24.145 --> 00:50:35.825
[Yashas]: In fact, it can be even bad or unnecessary, but it's just the people come together and talk about whatever they want to talk about.

00:50:35.825 --> 00:50:39.660
[Yashas]: It could be music, biology, future technology, whatever.

00:50:39.720 --> 00:50:40.620
[Kaspar]: König of a...

00:50:40.620 --> 00:50:41.120
[Kaspar]: Yeah.

00:50:41.260 --> 00:50:44.860
[Kaspar]: Feels like a like a basic need Colaboradio.

00:50:45.400 --> 00:50:47.120
[Kaspar]: Shetty you tell it like this, it like...

00:50:47.180 --> 00:50:47.680
[Yashas]: Yes.

00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:52.755
[Yashas]: We assume it is The, when we were...

00:50:52.755 --> 00:51:03.735
[Yashas]: Our ancestors in the Kalahari would say, let's go and collaborate on bringing that jeep, the giraffe back as meat.

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:05.700
[Yashas]: It wasn't...

00:51:06.480 --> 00:51:06.800
[Yashas]: It...

00:51:06.800 --> 00:51:07.440
[Yashas]: There was...

00:51:07.440 --> 00:51:12.180
[Yashas]: There were no grants to write, no elaborate planning.

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:15.940
[Yashas]: We shared whatever we got or whatever we did.

00:51:17.135 --> 00:51:20.415
[Kaspar]: Thank you so much, Yashas, for these, beautiful words.

00:51:20.415 --> 00:51:23.075
[Kaspar]: And I think that's a point you make.

00:51:23.215 --> 00:51:24.435
[Yashas]: Thank you so much.

00:51:25.135 --> 00:51:37.770
[Kaspar]: So you are listening to Clap2Collab, transmitting on DAB plus channel 7D in Berlin and channel 12D in Brandenburg, as well as colaboradio.org.

00:51:38.470 --> 00:51:48.195
[Kaspar]: You can listen to it on the www.hackteria.org, and we are basically contributing to DIWO, to the DIWO spirit, which is do it with others.

